#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Forced Landing Without Power — Instructional Walkthrough",
  unit: "Instructor Training | Syllabus Walkthrough 11 of 12 — maps to Ab Initio Phase 3, Lesson 3",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
  plan_type: "Instructor Training",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
Draft an original lesson plan for the forced-landing-without-power sequence (ABCDEF), referencing the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7), then deliver an instructor-standard ground briefing and in-flight lesson from that material, to a standard the IC could use with a real ab-initio student.
]

,overview: [
Maps to Ab Initio Phase 3, Lesson 3 (*"Forced Landing Without Power"* — see `phase3-lesson03-Forced Landing Without Power` for the ab-initio reference content, used only afterward to check the IC's own draft — the IC does not read this document before drafting). This lesson requires the IC to teach a memory-item sequence (ABCDEF) fluently from memory themselves before they can credibly coach a student through it — an IC who needs to think through the sequence while teaching it will visibly undermine the lesson.

This lesson introduces a second structural change (after Lesson 10's independent brief): rather than working from an existing GCSFT lesson plan at all, the IC drafts an original lesson plan for FLWOP/ABCDEF referencing the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7) — per Lesson 10 homework — then briefs from their own material. The RAAus syllabus sets out what must be covered; turning that regulatory reference into a complete, sequenced, briefable lesson plan is the IC's goal. ABCDEF itself is well known to the IC by this stage; the instructing skill under test here is lesson-plan authorship — can the IC structure a usable lesson plan from the syllabus reference alone, without a GCSFT template to copy, and does their own version hold up when compared against the real ab-initio source afterward. The in-flight component reverts to the usual pattern: the IT demonstrates the full sequence, and the IC recreates it.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- Recall the ABCDEF sequence and the 6-S field selection criteria fluently from memory (per source lesson plan)
- Recall the minimum components a GCSFT lesson plan needs to be usable by another instructor without further explanation (aim, learning outcomes, ground brief topics, common errors, standards for progression, safety)
- Recall how FLWOP/ABCDEF is described in the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7), and what it requires versus what it leaves to the instructor's own judgement
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Draft an original, usable lesson plan for the FLWOP/ABCDEF objective by referencing the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7) directly, without referencing the GCSFT ab-initio source lesson plan, then brief from it
- Teach the ABCDEF sequence using the demonstration–performance method, according the IC's lesson plan
- Coach the IT/student attempts with assistance fading across repetitions
- Teach field selection using the 6-S criteria and the high-key/low-key reference points clearly
- Coach a simulated student through the sequence when they struggle partway through, without simply supplying the answer
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- [HF/SA] Workload management - model that the MAYDAY call (D) and passenger brief (E) come after aircraft control and field selection are secured, not before
- [SA] Patter the "maintain a mental map of suitable fields" habit as a continuous, syllabus-wide skill, not one confined to this lesson
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7, Issue 7.1) — the FLWOP element. This is the IC's only reference for drafting their original lesson plan; do not substitute the GCSFT ab-initio lesson plan
- Ab initio source content in full: `phase3-lesson03-Forced Landing Without Power` — Theory Knowledge and Ground Brief Topics sections (IT reference only, for the post-brief comparison)
- FAA-H-8083-9A — Ch.4 (Teaching Process — the demonstration–performance method's "instructor supervision" phase is central here: coaching recall under simulated stress without simply feeding the answer)
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 40 min IC-drafted brief + IT critique \
Equipment required: VNC chart of training area, whiteboard, blank lesson plan template \

*IC-Drafted Brief* (40 min)
- Per Lesson 10 homework, the IC has drafted an original lesson plan for FLWOP/ABCDEF (aim, learning outcomes, ground brief topics, common errors, standards for progression, safety), referencing only the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7), without reading the ab-initio GCSFT source lesson plan
- IC delivers a full ground brief from their own draft: NTS, engine failure causes, ABCDEF sequence in full detail, emergency braking and ground loop awareness
- IT confirms the IC can recite ABCDEF fluently and correctly, unprompted, before the flight
- IT compares the IC's draft lesson plan against the ab-initio source lesson plan afterward and critiques any gaps
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor Trainer (IT)*]
- Confirm the IC drafted the FLWOP/ABCDEF lesson plan ahead of time from the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7), without having read the ab-initio GCSFT source
- Choose and play a student capability level for the IC's turn — consider role-playing a student who stalls partway through the sequence at least once, to test the IC's coaching technique
- Review weather, NOTAMs, and maintenance release with the IC
- Fly the full in-flight demonstration
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor Candidate (IC)*]
- Present pertinent weather, NOTAMs, and maintenance release items to the IT
- Conduct the pre-flight inspection and fuelling as required
- Deliver the ground brief from their own draft lesson plan
- Observe the IT's in-flight demonstration
- Deliver the in-flight demonstration to the IT-as-student
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*IT Demonstration*]
- Fly the full sequence from the ab-initio lesson plan with complete patter
#text(size:12pt)[*IC Recreation*]
- IC flies the identical sequence, delivering patter to the IT-as-student
- When the IT (playing the student) hesitates or stalls partway through the sequence, IC coaches recall using a prompt rather than supplying the next step outright, per the demonstration–performance model
]

,common_errors:[
- *RAAus syllabus wording copied verbatim rather than turned into a teachable lesson plan*: The syllabus states what must be covered, not a briefable sequence — the IC must structure, sequence, and patter it themselves, not just reformat the syllabus wording.
- *Checks (C) taught before Attitude/Best field are secure*: Reinforce the fixed order — this is the same sequencing discipline error the ab-initio lesson itself flags.
- *Coaching a struggling student by supplying the answer*: The demonstration–performance method calls for a prompt, not the answer — coach the IC toward this distinction.
- *MAYDAY calls not simulated fully*: It's important that the student practices these calls with the added pressure of multitasking. "I'll do a mayday call" doesn't assess the student's ability properly.
]

,post_flight:[
- *IC Self-Debrief*: IC critiques their own brief and demonstration.
- *IT Feedback*: Quality and completeness of the IC's original lesson plan against both the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7) requirements and the ab-initio source; fluency of the IC's own ABCDEF recall while teaching; correct sequencing in the taught content; quality of coaching when the simulated student struggled.
- *Next Steps*: Confirm readiness to progress to Unusual Attitudes and Emergencies walkthrough. Log the student capability played this session.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- IC's original lesson plan correctly reflects the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7) FLWOP requirements, not just the GCSFT ab-initio lesson plan's own framing of them
- IC's original lesson plan contains all required sections and would be usable by another instructor without further explanation, and holds up well against the ab-initio source when compared afterward
- IC recites and teaches ABCDEF fluently and in the correct order, unprompted
- IC teaches the 6-S field selection criteria and high-key/low-key reference points clearly
- IC coaches a stalled simulated student using a prompt rather than supplying the answer
- *Outcome*: Recorded in IC training record (NC/C, Instructor Training Syllabus Elements 5.1, 6.2, 6.4, 9.2, 9.3). "At or near" instructor standard is sufficient to progress.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Lesson can go ahead if conditions are VMC. IT can simulate a clear horizon if required. (ceiling >2500 ft AGL, surface wind \<25 kt).
- *Altitude*: Training go-around height specified before flight; minimum 500 ft AGL, never breached.
- *Emergency Procedures*: IT retains PIC authority throughout; if the exercise drifts toward the only available field with glide mismanaged, go around.
- *TEM*: IT-as-student and IT-as-instructor distinction made clearly with "student/instructor taking over" and correct control handover.
- *HF (4.1/4.4)*: IT confirms the IC managed session prep and time effectively (materials ready, brief timed, no last-minute scramble) and monitors both own and simulated-student fatigue/workload throughout, adjusting pace if either degrades.
- *Aircraft Limits*: As per ab-initio source lesson (clear engine every 1000 ft in descent).
]

,homework:[
- Review `phase3-lesson04-Unusual Attitudes and Emergencies` in full ahead of the next walkthrough lesson (note: the E10 scenario-stalling recap portion of that lesson is out of scope for this walkthrough — focus only on the E16 unusual-attitude and emergency content).
- Prepare brief notes on the nose-high and nose-low unusual attitude recovery sequences, and the ADM (Time → Assess → Predict → Evaluate) framework.
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
